content:
liam: quick share — ran an nsys + perfetto trace on the KV-heavy workload, saw weird kernel-selection chatter and lots of tiny kernel calls clustered per request. attaching trace: https://perfetto.local/trace/2178900001
ash: link looks good, I can open it. what's the main pain?
liam: the timeline shows ~8-12 tiny kernel launches right after KV prefetch, then a larger fused kernel. latency spikes correlate with the tiny ones.
ash: are those actual CUDA kernels or driver housekeeping?
kevin: looks like real kernels — our kernel-selector loop is launching short-lived kernels while it decides quant/strategy. NVTX ranges would help us attribute the decision time vs execution.
sophia: agreed — we should add NVTX ranges around the selector and around each candidate kernel invocation. naming needs to be stable for trace grouping.
jamal: proposal: add ranges like `RUNTIME:kernel_select`, `RUNTIME:kernel_call:<arch>:<kind>`, and `REQUEST:<req_id>:span` for top-level request. keep them short.
liam: +1 but `REQUEST:<req_id>` can be too long for filters. maybe `req.<shortid>` or add `req` as tag field?
kevin: NVTX has limited string dedupe but Perfetto grouping is easier if strings are standardized. I'd do `r.req.<sid>` for request and `k.sel` / `k.call.<name>` for kernels. Example: `k.call.flash_attn_fwd`.
sophia: concern: per-request spans for every request will generate a lot of NVTX pushes/pops. overhead measured? perfbot, what's our typical push/pop cost from last run?
perfbot: nvtx_lib push+pop cost (measured) ~120ns on our test nodes, 95% CI [100ns, 140ns]. Bulk of trace overhead comes from many short ranges.
jamal: 120ns is fine per request but not fine if we add 10-20 ranges per token loop. we should sample instrumentation in hot loops.
liam: sampling idea: always emit granular ranges when `env=INSTRUMENT=1` or when `rand() % N == 0` for low-overhead runs. Also add a lightweight integer tag for request id in a separate NVTX attribute to avoid long strings.
kevin: nvtxRangePushEx lets you set nvtxEventAttributes_t with payload U32. We can put a 32-bit request id there. then names can be generic for grouping and payload gives precise mapping. code snippet: ```c
nvtxEventAttributes_t attr = {0};
attr.version = NVTX_VERSION;
attr.size = NVTX_EVENT_ATTRIB_STRUCT_SIZE;
attr.colorType = NVTX_COLOR_ARGB;
attr.color = 0xFF4C8BF5;
attr.messageType = NVTX_MESSAGE_TYPE_ASCII;
attr.message.ascii = "k.call.flash_attn";
attr.payloadType = NVTX_PAYLOAD_TYPE_UNSIGNED_INT64;
attr.payload.u64 = (uint64_t)req_shortid;
nvtxRangePushEx(&attr);
// kernel launch
nvtxRangePop();
```
sophia: that looks good — minimal string churn and we can still read messages in trace UI. also put `k.sel` ranges around the selection loop with a counter tag for how many candidates tried.
liam: another ask: make parent/child spans explicit. currently kernels show as independent; if we push a top-level `r.req.<id>` before selection then push `k.sel` and then `k.call` as nested ranges, perfetto UI will show nesting. important for attribution.
jamal: agree. but careful: nested push/pop pattern must be balanced in error paths. add RAII helper in C++ to ensure pop on scope exit.
kevin: something like `AutoRange r("r.req", req_id)` that increments a static counter for dedupe and sets payload.
sophia: what about naming conventions doc? we should standardize and add a short list to the runtime README: prefix: `r.` for request, `k.` for kernel, `s.` for scheduler, `c.` for cache events. keep components 2-3 chars to keep traces readable.
liam: +1. also add a rule: no per-timestep unique strings (avoid `req_full_uuid` in names). long IDs go only in payload.
kevin: I've got a quick patch: adds `nvtx_utils::PushRange(name, req_id, u64 payload, color)` and `AutoNvtxRange` RAII. will open PR and link.
liam: cool — can you also add a flag to optionally enable full verbosity for smoke runs? perfbot: please run smoke traces on PR branch after CI landing.
perfbot: acknowledged. scheduled `nsys profile --sample=none --force-overwrite --trace=nvtx,cuda,osrt --output=patch-2178900001` once PR merges.
jamal: follow-ups: (1) add sampling config, (2) doc naming conventions, (3) RAII helper + request id payload, (4) run perfetto diff to ensure overhead acceptable.
sophia: noted. I'll draft naming doc and attach to PR.
liam: thanks all — aim to land tomorrow and run a 1h canary trace. :thumbsup:
